{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted","id":190934,"title":"More mummies wanted","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-07-07T17:37:42-07:00","id":190934,"title":"More mummies wanted","content":"I am wondering when Mummy is going to expand to include: 1) Native American mummies (Aztec &amp; Chinchorro), 2) European Mummies (Capuchin Monks), 3) Canary Islands (Guanche Culture), and Oceanic Mummies (Australia and the Torres Strait). Any possibility? I also find the idea that some cultures used natural environment in mummification possible also - China, Central Asia, Bog Mummies from Celtic Europe?, mounds in North America, etc. Any possible projects on this?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cIt was clear to me for a long time that the origins of science had their deep roots in a particular myth, that of invariance.&quot; ~ Giorgio de Santillana<br /> Preface to Hamlet's Mill","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2345-whithers","name":"Whithers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2345_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"141"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-07T18:01:54-07:00","id":190979,"title":"","content":"Nope.<br /> <br /> I'm struggling to find the archived post from the old forums, but suffice it to be said that Malcolm Sheppard has pointed out that <b>Mummy</b> is not a game about &quot;isn't it curious that English uses the same word for all these different kinds of preserved bodies?&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-07T18:15:15-07:00","id":190985,"title":"","content":"Not gonna happen, sadly. M:tC is firmly about the Nameless Empire and the long shadow it casts over history. Can your Mummy be low on Memory and had a cult in Mesoamerica, one that even provided him with a new body? Certainly. But no matter what, your character has to hail from Irem or somewhere close by. It's one of my main sticking points with the game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-07T20:41:43-07:00","id":191076,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Whithers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted#post190934\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am wondering when Mummy is going to expand to include: 1) Native American mummies (Aztec &amp; Chinchorro), 2) European Mummies (Capuchin Monks), 3) Canary Islands (Guanche Culture), and Oceanic Mummies (Australia and the Torres Strait). Any possibility? I also find the idea that some cultures used natural environment in mummification possible also - China, Central Asia, Bog Mummies from Celtic Europe?, mounds in North America, etc. Any possible projects on this?</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not who this game is about, but I have an NPC who's old body was destroyed millennia ago, and his Incan Cult sacrificed a girl to their god as a new body with all attendant rites. (S)he's integrated the two conflicting identities together, as the body brought its own memories in the deal.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-07T22:06:53-07:00","id":191121,"title":"","content":"Official word is something along the lines of &quot;Never say never, but that's not really what this game is about.&quot;<br /> <br /> If you want to be clever about it, embrace the identity crisis parts of Mummy. Where is the line drawn when it comes to one's identity? If a mummy's cult lands in Peru and stays there for generations upon generations, if the Arisen spends more time there and/or remembers more time spent there than he did in the City of Pillars when he was alive, if his original body was burned and one of his native Peruvian cultists provided him a new body and set of memories - if any of these things happen, or all of these, does he get to call himself Peruvian?<br /> <br /> If playing around with that idea doesn't work for you, you could always play with a variation of the Purified from WoD: Immortals, or come up with something yourself using M:tC as a baseline. If you want, you could always go with the idea that whatever powers <i>your</i> mummies is a bastardized version of the Rite of Return.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage the Awakening 2e Communal Homebrew Threads:<br /> - <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/966394-perils-of-the-path-post-your-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">Perils of the Path</a> - Other homebrew threads linked in the OP!<br /> <br /> Join us at the unofficial IRC channels on.irc.sorcery.net at #exalted and now #theonyxpath!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/451-kirby-jerusalem","name":"Kirby Jerusalem","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar451_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"589"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-08T08:49:26-07:00","id":191422,"title":"","content":"As everyone else has pointed out, MtC is pretty tied into the Nameless Empire plot. Mummy: The Resurrection dealt with mummies of the world, but I don't think MtR is nearly as good as MtC. <br /> <br /> That being said, I don't dislike MtR at all, so I wouldn't say no to a Mummy Translation Guide. That could provide a way to play other mummies of the world in the New setting. But whether that's a project the company is interested in doing is yet to be seen.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-08T14:37:35-07:00","id":191703,"title":"","content":"If you dig around, I did a post about how to work foreign angles for mummies. There are going to be new mummies in the sothis rising book, but Im guessing more Lifeless than deathless.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","name":"Cleverest of Things","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar935_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"650"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-09T08:42:57-07:00","id":192445,"title":"","content":"Even the corebook mentions that some of the human cultures following the Empire (which is to say, every society in history ever) tried to mimic the Rite. You have Egyptian mummies, of course, and who knows what necromancers tried to raise their sacred dead over the years? The Arisen themselves may have tried to recreate the Rite, travelling even to distant Peru before ocean- or air-transportation with the aid of relics and utterances.<br /> <br /> So your options, which I think are fair, are either Arisen who have lost a whole bunch of Memory and cultural identity or to use mockeries which I would only allow to be Lifeless. The Deathless state is something reserved for the Arisen and other Mummies who trace their origins to the Shan'iatu","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-09T11:14:35-07:00","id":192536,"title":"","content":"I would point toward things like the Purified for weird, mummy like beings which may showed up through history in different cultures. I assume that a mummy which meets a purified for the first time would be kinda surprised- unless she remembers meeting such creature in her &quot;previous life&quot;.<br /> <br /> EDIT- you can add the Osiris and the Mekhet for as examples for &quot;mummy like beings&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-13T03:47:33-07:00","id":196173,"title":"","content":"I was curious. I can see setting up the Guanche Culture mummies as derived from the same source as Egypt. Maybe the Capuchin Monks as well. However, the Chinchorro mummies were deliberately human prepared mummies that predate the Egyptian culture by a few thousand years. That sort of throws a kink in the they came from Egypt to Peru thing. The Aztec I am uncertain about as they are an Apathascan people, like the Navajo. The Australoids separated culturally from the rest of mankind around 50,000 BC. That also throws a slight kink in the temporal line from Egypt. (Besides, I think it would be interesting to see how Mummies from the land of Dreamtime would work!)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cIt was clear to me for a long time that the origins of science had their deep roots in a particular myth, that of invariance.&quot; ~ Giorgio de Santillana<br /> Preface to Hamlet's Mill","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2345-whithers","name":"Whithers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2345_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"141"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-13T08:19:50-07:00","id":196307,"title":"","content":"In those cases I see it less as the Arisen inspiring those practices, and more that the Arisen became immersed in that culture at a later date and co-opted it as part of their personality.<br /> <br /> Mummy as a rule seems to have a very strong fix on what it wants to be, from a cultural perspective. It's a game about Egyptian Mummies, and pulling from outside sources for new types kinda works against the central themes of the game. On the other hand, a Mummy who has lost enough Memory to believe they come from another culture is completely within the themes of the game, so long as you accept that said character will be wrong. I just wouldn't hold out hope that the developers will begin adding new creature types based on every culture in history that has at some point preserved its dead.<br /> <br /> Still, to add to the list of existing creatures you could use to represent Mummies from other cultures, you have Abmortals from Geist (pretty sure they could survive long enough and generally take the form of a preserved corpse) and whatever creature you decide the Blasphemous Depiction Effigy creates.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-13T08:27:48-07:00","id":196310,"title":"","content":"Mummy is not a game about playing Egyptian mummies; it's a game about the exploration of old age, and about playing characters who've been to countless places throughout 6000 years of existence, some of which they can even remember. The Nameless Empire need not necessarily be one of those.<br /> <br /> I advise using those 6000 long, cold years as much as possible in the context of a game - Mummy is about figuring out how the protagonists have spent the millenia, what sort of things they did, the cultures and peoples they met, etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/286-christian-a","name":"Christian A","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"832"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-14T07:41:45-07:00","id":196781,"title":"","content":"Actually, I would argue that Mummy is very much about playing Egyptian Mummies. Yes, thematically, it is completely about old age and the exploration of identity. A game is made of more than one aspect though, and I will disagree vehemently with anyone who claims aesthetics are an unimportant part of an experience. Mummy: The Curse's aesthetics are thoroughly Egyptian in nature, and that is every bit as much part of the experience as the questions imposed by Memory and the loss of power. The way in which you choose to frame a metaphor is important, just because it is a metaphor doesn't make the medium through which you tell it valueless. And from the five-fold soul, Utterances like Rite of the Sacred Scarab, ancient tombs and arcane geometry, and the myriad other pieces of flavour that evoke a feeling of Ancient Egypt it is quite, quite clear that Mummy: The Curse is a game about Egyptian Mummies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-14T17:35:44-07:00","id":197275,"title":"","content":"I'm not trying to throw out Christian A's comments, but once Malcom posted here saying very clearly the game was about 'Egyptian Mummies.'<br /> <br /> You're right, still, in that it's a game about characters who have all but unimaginable histories and experiences, some of which may overshadow all other significant events in their lives save perhaps the Rite of Return. Still, it's about invoking the classic Egyptian-style mummy; in that same thread Malcolm posted, I think, someone said something like, &quot;When you think of mummies as horror movie monsters do you think of them anything other than Egyptian?&quot;<br /> <br /> Anyway. I'm really excited and hoping to see <i>Sothis Ascends</i> and what sort of other mummified Lifeless there might be","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-14T18:23:16-07:00","id":197296,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted?p=196307#post196307\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I just wouldn't hold out hope that the developers will begin adding new creature types based on every culture in history that has at some point preserved its dead</div> </div> </div> </div>That is just it though. There are only a limited number of cultures that have ever mummified the dead. Preserving the dead is a different story. The entire list of those who specifically mummified their dead is six:<br /> Australians, diverged from global DNA about 30,000 BCE<br /> Chinchorro, earliest Atacama Dessert 7020 BCE<br /> Guanche, left the Sahara for the Canaries after 6,000 BCE<br /> Egypt, estimated to begin about 3300 BCE<br /> Mexica (Aztec), 600s to 1525<br /> Capuchin Monks, 1599 to the 1920s<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted?p=196781#post196781\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The way in which you choose to frame a metaphor is important, ...</div> </div> </div> </div>And it could well be that by framing the mechanics so clearly in one specific culture, including the others simply ceases to be possible.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>reseru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted?p=197275#post197275\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">... I think, someone said something like, &quot;When you think of mummies as horror movie monsters do you think of them anything other than Egyptian?&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>The Robot vs. the Aztec Mummy, 1958 film<br /> Kolchak: The Night Stalker: Season 1, Episode 17 - Legacy of Terror, 1975<br /> Lone Ranger and Tonto, Issue #4, The Last Battle, Nov 1994<br /> Mil Mascaras vs. the Aztec Mummy, 2007 American Lucha Libre film<br /> <br /> And yes, Joan Rivers and I were at the caveman picnic watching the first dinosaur come out of its egg!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cIt was clear to me for a long time that the origins of science had their deep roots in a particular myth, that of invariance.&quot; ~ Giorgio de Santillana<br /> Preface to Hamlet's Mill","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2345-whithers","name":"Whithers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2345_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"141"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-14T22:10:59-07:00","id":197525,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Whithers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted?p=197296#post197296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And it could well be that by framing the mechanics so clearly in one specific culture, including the others simply ceases to be possible.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I don't disagree, but I don't see that as a negative thing. A Mummy can be immersed into another culture and come to think of itself as part of that culture, but it will never be anything but wrong. That doesn't necessarily stop you from playing an &quot;Aztec&quot; Mummy, so long as you accept that your character will eventually find out that they aren't Aztec at all. However, some games benefit from having a narrow focus and I believe Mummy: The Curse is one of these games.<br /> <br /> Some of the new Ghost rules make it very easy to create undead from other cultures though. They won't be quite as powerful as Arisen, but with the right Numen you can have an animated corpse with the ability to reinforce its stats, regenerate damage, and an Age trait that works like a power trait for the purpose of resisting magic, plus a few other magical abilities of your choice. With the Lucid Dead Numen, such creatures are even playable characters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-14T22:12:38-07:00","id":197526,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Whithers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted?p=197296#post197296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Robot vs. the Aztec Mummy, 1958 film<br /> Kolchak: The Night Stalker: Season 1, Episode 17 - Legacy of Terror, 1975<br /> Lone Ranger and Tonto, Issue #4, The Last Battle, Nov 1994<br /> Mil Mascaras vs. the Aztec Mummy, 2007 American Lucha Libre film</div> </div> </div> </div>While I'm impressed because I had never heard of these, neither the poster whom I was paraphrasing or myself were saying there were zero examples of non-Egyptian mummies, but rather that the vast, vast majority of pop culture and public awareness is that your stereotypical, dare I say cliched, horror movie mummy is Egyptian. The fact is that Universal Pictures put out half a dozen films about it back in the last century, and more recently had a remake of them that we all know and love (which did feature a Chinese 'mummy') and that has another remake in the making, too!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":638,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","user":"reseru","date":".  impressed* rather than pressed 07-16-2014, 08:09 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-14T22:47:48-07:00","id":197564,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted?p=197525#post197525\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't disagree, but I don't see that as a negative thing. A Mummy can be immersed into another culture and come to think of itself as part of that culture, but it will never be anything but wrong. That doesn't necessarily stop you from playing an &quot;Aztec&quot; Mummy, so long as you accept that your character will eventually find out that they aren't Aztec at all. However, some games benefit from having a narrow focus and I believe Mummy: The Curse is one of these games.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not really true, what with the second rebirth.<br /> <br /> An iremite mummy gets their body destroyed and their cult provides them with, using your example, an aztec body.<br /> <br /> Nahuatl becomes their native language, they can now remember growing up as an Aztec, their family, their wife, the modern day Aztec descendants can include their descendants. <br /> <br /> The fact that they also have an origin in ancient Irem with memories that extend from before the Aztecs were a people doesn't invalidate the fact that they also have an Aztec origin now as well, and that this origin could have far more importance in a game since Irem is a barely remembered city that fell 6000 years ago, no mummy has tracked down a descendants through 6000 years of genealogy, there are no Iremite-American holidays.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-14T23:39:04-07:00","id":197608,"title":"","content":"The Twice-Arisen are a bit more complicated, yes. The only Twice-Arisen character we've seen in official material still identifies as Iremite though. It certainly makes the issue more complicated, but the origins of the Mummy (as in, the magic that created and sustains them) is still Iremite in nature rather than Aztec. So while the character might identify as an Aztec person, his nature as a Mummy is still proto-Egyptian.<br /> <br /> I do largely agree with you though. The ability to become Twice-Arisen allows for a more complicated identity, but the aesthetics of the supernatural aspects are still Egyptian.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-15T04:16:14-07:00","id":197760,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>reseru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted?p=197526#post197526\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While I'm pressed because I had never heard of these, neither the poster whom I was paraphrasing or myself were saying there were zero examples of non-Egyptian mummies, but rather that the vast, vast majority of pop culture and public awareness is that your stereotypical, dare I say cliched, horror movie mummy is Egyptian. The fact is that Universal Pictures put out half a dozen films about it back in the last century, and more recently had a remake of them that we all know and love (which did feature a Chinese 'mummy') and that has another remake in the making, too!</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I know what your saying. And that <a href=\"http://www.imdb.com/title/tt0103859/\" target=\"_blank\">Boomerang</a> just keeps going round. And what I really liked about the movie Boomerang were the breaks in the cliches. There were maybe 3 token white people in it, while the hero was trying to sleep his way to the top. But really what is the WoD? Is it merely a whitebread tradition seasoned with a smattering of colored sugar dots? And I know it comes from my bad attitude as an &quot;American&quot;, but <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/redface.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Embarrassment\" smilieid=\"2\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> To be an American is to be the child of criminals, malcontents, and refugees whose blood and bone of kith and kin are scattered like the dew on every inch of every land where any man has died without dignity, where any woman has wept for survival, where any child has lost the sanctity of home for the slavery of savagely imposed life to the whip of tyrants, the strangling coils of war, and that humanity which calls others lesser or property. For me it is not a question of the stereotypical because I know one person's stereotype is another's role model. For me it is the revelation that I am man! And every heritage, every blood, every savage light and silent night is mine. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> You know - it's the heavy crap.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>StSword</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted?p=197564#post197564\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The fact that they also have an origin in ancient Irem with memories that extend from before the Aztecs were a people doesn't invalidate the fact that they also have an Aztec origin now as well, and that this origin could have far more importance in a game since Irem is a barely remembered city that fell 6000 years ago, no mummy has tracked down a descendants through 6000 years of genealogy, there are no Iremite-American holidays.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is likely the Guanche and Egyption derived from the same Proto-Egyption source. The Mexica (Aztec) could fit in the Egyption timeline, but mummify in completely different postures and practices. The Capuchin Monks certainly fit in the Egyption timeline and could have had access to their tradition in records from the Roman period. But the Chinchorro and the Indigenous Australians come before Egyption tradition. The first by thousands of years, the second potentially by tens of thousands of years. No other peoples practiced deliberate mummification according to the records I have seen. All other mummies are the result of natural environment.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cIt was clear to me for a long time that the origins of science had their deep roots in a particular myth, that of invariance.&quot; ~ Giorgio de Santillana<br /> Preface to Hamlet's Mill","edited":{"userid":2345,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2345-whithers","user":"Whithers","date":". 07-15-2014, 07:19 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2345-whithers","name":"Whithers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2345_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"141"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-15T08:26:39-07:00","id":197938,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Whithers</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">But the Chinchorro and the Indigenous Australians come before Egyption tradition. The first by thousands of years,</div> </div> </div> </div>Bear in mind that the proto-egyptian Nameless Empire also predates the culture we think of as &quot;ancient egypt&quot; (which is to say, Kemet) by a millenium or two.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">A Mummy can be immersed into another culture and come to think of itself as part of that culture, but it will never be anything but wrong. That doesn't necessarily stop you from playing an &quot;Aztec&quot; Mummy, so long as you accept that your character will eventually find out that they aren't Aztec at all.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that depends really heavily on what you mean by &quot;being&quot; of that culture. If I've lived in a place for longer than most of the people there have been alive, if I know the culture of that place inside out and am accepted by that culture as a member (or even a respected elder or divine being), and I identify with that culture, does my memory of being part of some other culture <i>first</i> really negate that?<br /> <br /> I think there's plenty of room for a mummy to remember her origins and <i>still</i> consider herself part of, for example, an indigenous south american culture. Or for that matter to consider themselves a USian. Or whatever. Origins don't dictate identity, they just inform it.<br /> <br /> (And now I am imagining a super patriotic USian mummy being all &quot;Son, it doesn't matter that I came from Egypt, your ancestors came from Ireland and the Sudan and Germany. I was there when Lee surrendered and I've been voting since the 15th amendment, I dare you to tell me I'm not an American&quot;)<br /> <br /> Edit: Actually, the longer I think about it the more I think that the mummy who mistakenly believes he has <i>always</i> been Aztec would be an absurd rarity. Memory of basic identity stuff like &quot;I am a servant of the nameless empire&quot; and &quot;I am a member of X Guild&quot; is some of the last stuff to go when memory lowers. So a mummy who believed something counter to those facts would almost need to be straight up delusional, rather than just being forgetful.<br /> <br /> On the flipside, high-memory mummies are simultaneously more likely to clearly remember being from egypt, and also (by virtue of thinking more like humans) more likely to want to identify with a nearby, living culture. Knowing you're a citizen of the nameless empire doesn't help you much when it's millenia dead and you crave all the things (like community, belonging, ritual) that come with actually being immersed and identified with a living culture. While low memory mummies are probably content to just ignore that and do their jobs, the high memory ones are probably going to integrate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":996,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/996-gabby","user":"Gabby","date":". 07-15-2014, 02:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/996-gabby","name":"Gabby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-15T13:28:04-07:00","id":198152,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gabby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted?p=197938#post197938\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Bear in mind that the proto-egyptian Nameless Empire also predates the culture we think of as &quot;ancient egypt&quot; (which is to say, Kemet) by a millenium or two.</div> </div> </div> </div>Even assuming that the Nameless Empire coexisted with the Chinchorro, it is also 7,630 miles/12,280 km between Cairo and the Atacama Desert. It is 7,198 miles/11,584 km beteen Cairo and the Tiwi Islands, Australia. Further, indigenous DNA indicates no outside influences for over 30,000 years between the Australoids and the rest of humanity until the European Expansion. Nor is there any DNA evidence of relationship between North Africa and the Chinchorro. I'm just saying that even if you derive all other ritual mummification from the Nameless Empire, there are at least two examples that 1) Predate Egypt, 2) are incredibly remote, and 3) have completely different cultural flavorings from the back-storied Nameless Empire.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cIt was clear to me for a long time that the origins of science had their deep roots in a particular myth, that of invariance.&quot; ~ Giorgio de Santillana<br /> Preface to Hamlet's Mill","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2345-whithers","name":"Whithers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2345_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"141"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-15T18:41:56-07:00","id":198442,"title":"","content":"All that aside. Even before you get into alternate bodies. The mummy who has spent the vast majority of their time in say China and whose memories, rituals, cults,etc have been cultivated there for a millennia. The meaning of ultimately being from Irem is quite diminished in a lot of ways. Yes it's not quite what OP wants but I think it's something that should not be overlooked either.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-16T00:26:06-07:00","id":198640,"title":"","content":"I have in argument conceded most of the worlds mummies cannot qualify, as they are naturally occurring. The only cultures I continued to bring up are the ones that actually practiced deliberate mummification.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cIt was clear to me for a long time that the origins of science had their deep roots in a particular myth, that of invariance.&quot; ~ Giorgio de Santillana<br /> Preface to Hamlet's Mill","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2345-whithers","name":"Whithers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2345_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"141"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-17T01:20:15-07:00","id":199792,"title":"","content":"The thing is though is that mummies from other cultures don't actually have any particular place in the game. The cursed are proto-egyptians in eternal servitude to their Judge for feeding them relics, so what would a peruvian mummy have to do with any of that? It would be as much of a crossover if you had the mummies hang out with the purified.<br /> <br /> Not that I see that as a bad thing, but mummies from other cultures actually belongs more as a generic blue book supplement than a mummy supplement, at least in wicked dead the other vampires are things that would fight over resources or endanger the masquerade.<br /> <br /> But a bog man? As much as I think it would be pretty cool as a splat, it's doubtful they spend their time hunting relics, and while they might form cults as a matter of course so does practically every other splat in the world of darkness, so a mummy cult getting into a conflict with a bog man cult would be as much as a crossover as if they were tangling with a blood cult, a demon cult, a godmachine cult, a sin-eater cult, a bloodbather cult, changeling cult, a sin-eater cult, etc, etc.<br /> <br /> So if they did a wicked dead book of alternate mummies, it would fit better as a blue book, not a mummy book in my opinion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-17T08:13:43-07:00","id":200043,"title":"","content":"It's funny you cite <i>Wicked Dead</i> because that's what I used to say would be a perfect type fit for <i>Mummy</i>. It's too bad they already did <i>Immortals</i> because it's those types of supernaturals that concern Mummies the most besides what's otherwise in the <i>Mummy </i>line - not that you can't still use the supplement, but still. Changeling or Mage or whatever cults are nice for little stories and short campaigns, but they probably won't even be around, and certainly won't have the same membership or goals or motives probably, the next time a Mummy wakes up. Vampires, blood-bathers, eternal wardens, and other immortals can go through the ages with Mummies.<br /> <br /> So a book - geared towards <i>Mummy</i>, mind you, rather than the blue book <i>Immortals</i> - filled with immortals and cultural mummified Lifeless would be great. Maybe there wouldn't be much of a difference, but it makes sense in my head <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-18T10:13:02-07:00","id":201241,"title":"","content":"As an example, the Chinchorro have different periods of mummies. What if the Red Mummy period was a more realistic or positive period of dedication. However, the Black Mummy period was MtC - with mummies created by priests of the Wyrm?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cIt was clear to me for a long time that the origins of science had their deep roots in a particular myth, that of invariance.&quot; ~ Giorgio de Santillana<br /> Preface to Hamlet's Mill","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2345-whithers","name":"Whithers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2345_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"141"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-19T06:42:58-07:00","id":202055,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Whithers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted#post190934\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am wondering when Mummy is going to expand to include: 1) Native American mummies (Aztec &amp; Chinchorro), 2) European Mummies (Capuchin Monks), 3) Canary Islands (Guanche Culture), and Oceanic Mummies (Australia and the Torres Strait). Any possibility? I also find the idea that some cultures used natural environment in mummification possible also - China, Central Asia, Bog Mummies from Celtic Europe?, mounds in North America, etc. Any possible projects on this?</div> </div> </div> </div>I dont like these 'no' or 'nope' or 'not gonna happen' answers, they are not inclusive. Instead what about finding work arounds?<br /> <br /> Nothing to keep your Mummy from having migrated to those parts of the world, even adopted some of the local customs; adopted some of the local religoius trappings, even worshipped as a local deity; however, ultimately, they are still unique to the Rite of Return which is only available to proto-Egyptian Irem (just like Mages harken back to Atlantis). I am sure mummies had their cults move and their vessels kept in tombs in Meso america and even artic regions.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Assistant Organizer for:</b><br /> <a href=\"http://www.meetup.com/Dead-Gamers-Society/\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i162.photobucket.com/albums/t261/jlburgos_bucket/a41e98d1-1eed-4506-a8d2-2b3bea4a43da_zps469395ad.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a>","edited":{"userid":311,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/311-joeb","user":"JoeB","date":". 07-19-2014, 09:45 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/311-joeb","name":"JoeB","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar311_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"198"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-19T07:01:00-07:00","id":202073,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Whithers</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted?p=198152#post198152\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Even assuming that the Nameless Empire coexisted with the Chinchorro, it is also 7,630 miles/12,280 km between Cairo and the Atacama Desert. It is 7,198 miles/11,584 km beteen Cairo and the Tiwi Islands, Australia. Further, indigenous DNA indicates no outside influences for over 30,000 years between the Australoids and the rest of humanity until the European Expansion. Nor is there any DNA evidence of relationship between North Africa and the Chinchorro. I'm just saying that even if you derive all other ritual mummification from the Nameless Empire, there are at least two examples that 1) Predate Egypt, 2) are incredibly remote, and 3) have completely different cultural flavorings from the back-storied Nameless Empire.</div> </div> </div> </div>As others have pointed out, such mummies have little to do with the (dare I say it?) metaplot of MtC. The defining trait of the Nameless Empire wasn\u2019t mummification; it was the dark sorceries of the Shan\u2019iatu (sp?). That\u2019s also the central feature of MtC. If you <i>were</i> to do something with one of the other cultures you\u2019ve mentioned, you\u2019d need to come up with a compelling counterpart to the Arisens\u2019 ancient lords. The resulting mummies would need to differ from the Arisen in basic theme, as in \u201csomething other than Deathless servants of ancient masters, repeatedly brought back to life to collect trinkets for them\u201d. Ultimately, it would be a separate game, more like <b>Kindred of the East</b> was for <b>Vampire: the Masquerade</b>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-19T13:46:15-07:00","id":202349,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted?p=202073#post202073\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As others have pointed out, such mummies have little to do with the (dare I say it?) metaplot of MtC.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm fulfilling this month's quota of nitpicking with this post, but &quot;history&quot; or &quot;background&quot; are probably better words than metaplot. Metaplot implies that the day zero of the game is constantly shifting forward in light of new events, where the stuff with the Rite of Return and all that are all way back and don't really affect the details of what happens when you set a game at a certain place or time.<br /> <br /> themoreyouknow.jpg<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage the Awakening 2e Communal Homebrew Threads:<br /> - <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/966394-perils-of-the-path-post-your-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">Perils of the Path</a> - Other homebrew threads linked in the OP!<br /> <br /> Join us at the unofficial IRC channels on.irc.sorcery.net at #exalted and now #theonyxpath!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/451-kirby-jerusalem","name":"Kirby Jerusalem","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar451_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"589"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-19T14:39:20-07:00","id":202395,"title":"","content":"I think Dataweaver knew the uncertainty of using the word 'metaplot' by his self-conscious preface right before the actual word.<br /> <br /> And I really like that idea, that there may have been other contemporary societies who had a variety of sorcerer-priest-kings as well. Just as, to me, any of the world's other walking mummies would be Lifeless, these other sorcerers, while still significantly powerful, still would live in the shadow of the Shan'iatu. After all, if these other Lifeless mummies from other cultures are walking around, someone must have created them.<br /> <br /> Perhaps that's what the Oracles of Ubar were, after all (talked about in the opening history of the Empire in the player's section)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-20T03:17:56-07:00","id":202787,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JoeB</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted?p=202055#post202055\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">... they are not inclusive. Instead what about finding work arounds?</div> </div> </div> </div>With this we are in agreement. One of the blessings of the CWoD was the inclusivity of the world. The current metas appear to have the purpose of lessening the inclusiveness (and with it the expansiveness) of the WoD game. What is purely, imho, a logistical and bottom-line decision has the unfortunate side effect of limiting the WoD to the metaplots which current cultures are expected to comfortably fit within their entertainment experience.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JoeB</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted?p=202055#post202055\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nothing to keep your Mummy from having migrated to those parts of the world, even adopted some of the local customs; adopted some of the local religious trappings, even worshipped as a local deity; ...</div> </div> </div> </div>What prevents this is timing. These other civilizations existed first. Even with the most powerful mages of CWoD breaking time backwards was strictly impossible. (Only one clan had the potential, and they still failed to save their founder.) The real questions are: What was so horrid? What was so powerful? What and why were the entities which so terrified the masters of Irem that they fled thousands of miles through the near Umbra to Egypt? Could they first have fled from Melanesia to Chile?<br /> <br /> One possibility lies in the heritage of headhunting practiced by the Jivaroan tribes. I remember being taught how the Inca regularly waged war to annihilate these tribes for practicing headshrinking. The Inca believed these practices destroyed the soul. Certainly, this would be a potent motivator for Pre-proto-Egyptian rulers to consider relocating. As I recall, headhunting was also practiced throughout the Torres Straits and regions of Polynesia and Melanesia.<br /> <br /> Quoting from <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shrunken_head\" target=\"_blank\">Wikipedia</a>:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <i>The practice of preparing shrunken heads originally had religious significance; shrinking the head of an enemy was believed to harness the spirit of that enemy and compel him to serve the shrinker. It was said to prevent the soul from avenging his death.<br /> <br /> Shuar believed in the existence of three fundamental spirits:</i><ul><li><i>Wakani - innate to humans thus surviving their death.</i></li> <li><i>Arutam - literally &quot;vision&quot; or &quot;power&quot;, protects humans from a violent death.</i></li> <li><i>Muisak - vengeful spirit, which surfaces when a person carrying an arutam spirit is murdered.</i></li> </ul><i>To block a Muisak from using its powers, they severed their enemies' heads and shrank them. The process also served as a way of warning their enemies. Despite these precautions, the owner of the trophy did not keep it for long. Many heads were later used in religious ceremonies and feasts that celebrated the victories of the tribe. Accounts vary as to whether the heads would be discarded or stored.</i> </div> </div> </div>The Chango people, on whom I can find very little currently, appear to be the remains of the once great Chinchorro civilization.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JoeB</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted?p=202055#post202055\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">... (just like Mages hearken back to Atlantis).</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, the temporary and illusory imprisonment of all mystical forces into this pocket realm delusion derived from Plato's Parable of Atlantis (about how the weight of ever expanding government collapses in upon and overwhelms the very government that generates it) is a toxic fiction by which truly enlightened mages have never been imprisoned. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />\u200b<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted?p=202073#post202073\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The resulting mummies would need to differ from the Arisen in basic theme, as in \u201csomething other than Deathless servants of ancient masters, repeatedly brought back to life to collect trinkets for them\u201d. Ultimately, it would be a separate game, ...</div> </div> </div> </div>A separate game implies separate mechanics; To that I would be opposed. Stir-fry can be done with the greatest variety of ingredients, and I also believe the WoD can do the same. However, it is clear that the Chinchorro practiced mummification for different reasons. They practiced it without limitation of class, wealth, age, gender, etc. Perhaps the flight of the founders of Irem had to do with the Mobocracy of the land they fled? Perhaps they were a minority with good reason to be distrustful of the evils of the democratic spirit and its mediocritizing pressures. Combine this with assaults by neighboring peoples capable of destroying the soul of the defeated through headshrinking and we create enemies both within and without. The question then becomes, how were their rites adapted to the religion and environment of the Sahara in the days before all its green was gone?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kirby Jerusalem</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted?p=202349#post202349\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm fulfilling this month's quota of nitpicking with this post, but &quot;history&quot; or &quot;background&quot; are probably better words than metaplot. Metaplot implies that the day zero of the game is constantly shifting forward in light of new events, where the stuff with the Rite of Return and all that are all way back and don't really affect the details of what happens when you set a game at a certain place or time.</div> </div> </div> </div>As an historian, I would have to disagree. As I use it: Metaplot (n) 1) A set of events deriving from a cause or group of causes which influence, or sometimes define, how the physical and intellectual resources will be framed in the expression of one or more individuals whose behavior is replicated in whole or in part by those who experience it.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cIt was clear to me for a long time that the origins of science had their deep roots in a particular myth, that of invariance.&quot; ~ Giorgio de Santillana<br /> Preface to Hamlet's Mill","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2345-whithers","name":"Whithers","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2345_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"141"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-20T07:45:07-07:00","id":202921,"title":"","content":"I gotta say, I feel like Ghost Numen (including the new ones from Mummy) provide all the powers you could possibly want or need to build other-culture Mummies. They can animate a mummified corpse as either a Revenant or an Intruder, reinforce its attributes, walk it through walls, fly, move things with their mind, create horrific phantasms, control animals, light fires, and so on. There is no limit preventing them from predating the Arisen, and with the Age Supernatural trait you can also make them highly resistant to other powers (anything more than 1,000 years old has Age 10, if I remember right). They can even be playable characters using the Lucid Dead Numen.<br /> <br /> The beauty of the nWoD is its toolbox nature, I find. You can take the elements and aspects that you want. Ghosts have none of the backstory that the Arisen have that requires them to be tied to Ancient Egypt, so it's entirely possible that as the Arisen spread across the world they could encounter even more ancient, mummified creatures that could certainly terrify a low-Sekhem Mummy. The Arisen are fundamentally tied to proto-Egypt, but if you want to build a mummy-like creature from another civilisation you have all tools you need to make one without any need for further official material.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-20T09:09:31-07:00","id":203002,"title":"","content":"In my game's history, the sorcerers had scried information about the chinchorro mummies fairly early in Irem's history. Id even considered an extreme leap--that the su-menent assigned to these studies were eventually mummified and entombed in south america (using a relic like the teleporation scroll). Of course, Ive been fairly generous with the extent of Irem, such as a tomb created in Siberia to house relics as far from Irem as they could get, or a handful of non-iremite races (a pale northern barbarian , for example) having had at least one member mummified. My Irem was trading with India and China, made war at the black sea and caucasus mountains, and had begun explorations, from its western colony, of western europe.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","name":"Cleverest of Things","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar935_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"650"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-22T07:04:02-07:00","id":204910,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure how to really address the basic of the OP's original question. As that's been answered basically (with a resounding NO Mummy the Curse is not doing the &quot;Mummy's from every culture that ever mummified a body thing&quot;) and moved on from.<br /> <br /> So with the idea of an &quot;official&quot; expansion to the line including the type of &quot;non-Iremite&quot; Mummy out-the-window we are firmly in the realms of &quot;in your game&quot;. The simple answer to that is - Yes, there can be Mummy's from other cultures and times in your game. Mainly because if you are choosing to do your own thing with your Mummy game, that's up to you and it really does not matter what other people think or say or do. If you want to change Mummy and run it more &quot;multi-cultural&quot; fine, go for it, have fun with that. <br /> <br /> All I'll say is that Sothis Ascends might feature some of the &quot;other Mummy's&quot; in it. Though they likely will be derivatives and bastardizations of the Rite of Return and almost certainly Lifeless. I guess its the closest we get to a Wicked Dead style book that Reseru mentioned. I'm sure with a bit of work they could be made into less antagonistic versions that might be PC material. Add in the Purified from Immortals and bigo, already got a bevy of Mummy's from other cultures that are not from Irem. <br /> <br /> Obviously, take the above with a pinch of salt, Sothis is still months from completion and we don't really have any idea about what these &quot;other mummy's&quot; its likely to feature are like at current. <br /> <br /> One thing I have noticed though is that the OP seems to be making an argument that the CWoD was more &quot;mulit-cultural&quot; then the NWoD, which I think is a whole other argument and, in my opinion, not really the right term. I'd say that the CWoD was more &quot;culturally-fetishistic&quot; then the NWoD, one only has to look at Kindred of the East to see that (its take on Asia is...yeah, and lets not get into the racial implication of the way the 1,000 Hell's spreads). NWoD is more about supernatural beings as archetypes that while having roots in a particular culture are abstracted out to be &quot;generic&quot; enough to fit into all cultures.<br /> <br /> For example, Mages in NWoD started in Atlantis which is a Greeko-Roman myth, but Mage's Atlantis only really shares a name with Plato's story, its not that Mage Atlantis was Greeko-Roman, it belonged to no culture, it was its own culture distinct from any present in the world today. <br /> <br /> Another example is the Clans of Vampire the Requiem, largely based off the European idea of a Vampire (and the 19th-20the Century idea of a Vampire as well). However, between the large archetypes of each clan, the numerous bloodlines and the &quot;other vampires&quot; detailed in splat books for the line, you can pretty much play any time of vampiric creature with a bit of rules tweaking. <br /> <br /> The other aspect we need to address is that some splats in the NWoD simply don't really draw on any real world culture. Werewolves and Demons for example have their own cosmology and metaphysical structure that might resemble some human cultures (shamanic traditions in the case of Werewolf) but are distinct from them. After all know one has a culture involved with the God-Machine in the real world. We hope...<br /> <br /> Still other splats are very, very multi-cultural by their nature - Hunters come from all walks of life and all cultures, Sin-Eaters attach to death mythology the world over, Prometheans tie into various cultural myths about created creatures. Once again, it could be said they tie into the larger multi-cultural narratives of folk lore and supernatural belief schema's that exist in a consistent cross-cultural narrative. <br /> <br /> The final point to make in regards to the NWoD approach to &quot;supernatural culture&quot; is that it is influenced by more then just cultural backdrop. Fictional narrative plays a roll as well (more so in some games then in others see Vampire vs Werewolf), as does distribution dynamics. So yes, Vampire the Requiem plays vampires as having elements of Bram Stoker and Ann Rice. Mummy has mummy's that hail from a proto-ancient Egypt. One type of Promethean is the Frankenstein. <br /> <br /> This is because the developers are influenced by media and know that their audience is also familiar with this media. So they use the common tropes and ideas in these popular and culturally prevalent (in the west and increasingly globally) takes on classic folklore as a sort of &quot;short-hand&quot;. Furthermore, these ideas are kinda ingrained in our social consciousness about these creatures these days. If I walked up to a person on the street and asked &quot;What are the things that make a Vampire a Vampire?&quot; or &quot;Where do Mummy's come from?&quot; I imagine I'd get a list of traits that describe Dracula in the former and Egypt for the latter. <br /> <br /> Some people might think that last point about being influenced by popular (Western) culture is a bad thing. I don't, but that's just my opinion. <br /> <br /> Ah, yes and as a final thing for the OP to think about. I notice a lot of CWoD getting thrown around in his posts (Wyrm, Near Umbra etc) so I was wondering if you were thinking of adapting Mummy the Curse into CWoD. If that's the case, awesome, go for it. <br /> <br /> \u200bThough could you please spare the people on a NWoD board the veiled hints about some CWoD being superior to their NWoD iterations. Your comments about cultural myopia and &quot;real&quot; mages (Ascension I take it?) not buying into the Atlantis thing seem to point towards this view point (though I could be misreading you, if so I apologize). I'm simply saying this because the whole &quot;Classic vs New&quot; WoD argument is old, worn out and utterly pointless, we all have bits we like more from each, its not something that needs to be part of forum posts that are nothing to do with that debate.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#0000cd\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><i>&quot;There is no God in the Badlands.&quot; </i></span></span></span><span style=\"font-family:helvetica, arial, sans-serif\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\">- Sunny</span></span><br /> <br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/a/img41/8167/obcp.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/599-hawthorn","name":"Hawthorn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar599_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"511"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-07-22T23:44:38-07:00","id":205779,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>reseru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/190934-more-mummies-wanted?p=200043#post200043\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's funny you cite <i>Wicked Dead</i> because that's what I used to say would be a perfect type fit for <i>Mummy</i>. It's too bad they already did <i>Immortals</i> because it's those types of supernaturals that concern Mummies the most besides what's otherwise in the <i>Mummy </i>line - not that you can't still use the supplement, but still. Changeling or Mage or whatever cults are nice for little stories and short campaigns, but they probably won't even be around, and certainly won't have the same membership or goals or motives probably, the next time a Mummy wakes up. Vampires, blood-bathers, eternal wardens, and other immortals can go through the ages with Mummies.<br /> <br /> So a book - geared towards <i>Mummy</i>, mind you, rather than the blue book <i>Immortals</i> - filled with immortals and cultural mummified Lifeless would be great. Maybe there wouldn't be much of a difference, but it makes sense in my head <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div> Well as I indicated, I'd buy a book of other mummies without a doubt, it just wouldn't be too useful for mummy the curse.<br /> <br /> What would be most useful type of immortals for mummy would be immortals one can see in a subordinate position to a mummy.<br /> <br /> A mummy turning a trusted high priest or friend into a purified instead of one of their own lesser type of immortal minion? I can see that.<br /> <br /> A mummy arranging for their cult to be a bunch of blood bathers or body thieves so that the mummy doesn't have to worry about constantly replacing the cult member to old age? Sure.<br /> <br /> But a mummy having a cup of tea with a peruvian mummy inbetween doing the cults bidding and finding relics for the Judges is no more organic than if it were a vampire or a changeling with that goblin vow or one of the demon possessed etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}